bank   Space shooter
clock  1512000
tick   220

category Weapons

sound Ship fire
audf  10 1 +3 56
audc  170 5 -1 11
amp   0.45
note  SOUND1 (A35131.1A) latches SHPFIR on and loads SND1 with $10 -- sixteen frames, 256
note  ms, which is what these 56 ticks are. That is a long gate for a shot, and it is why
note  the hardware's own envelope decay does the shaping: the volume fade here stands in
note  for it. Both fire sounds run through the same routine and the same length.

sound Saucer fire
audf  40 2 +7 28
audc  41 5 -1 10
amp   0.40
note  The same sixteen frames through the same SOUND1 call with X=1 instead of X=0 -- the
note  game distinguishes the two shots by which latch it writes, not by length. Lower and
note  coarser here, on the 5-bit poly, which is the usual way to say "not yours".

category Movement

sound Ship thrust
audf  200 8 0 16
audc  137 128 0 1
amp   0.35
note  SHPSND is held on for as long as the thrust switch is, and cleared the moment the
note  ship is not alive (STA A,SHPSND at the 25$ label). So it has no envelope of its own:
note  this is half a second of the loop, and a game holds it or retriggers it.

sound Saucer, large
audf  150 6 0 1
audf  120 6 0 1
audf  150 6 0 1
audf  120 6 0 1
audf  150 6 0 1
audf  120 6 0 1
audf  150 6 0 1
audf  120 6 0 1
audc  41 48 0 1
amp   0.40
note  SCRSND is gated on while a saucer is alive and SCRSEL takes D7 of the saucer's own
note  size byte, so large and small differ only in what the analog circuit is switched to.
note  The alternation is the character of it: two dividers swapped every six ticks.

sound Saucer, small
audf  70 5 0 1
audf  55 5 0 1
audf  70 5 0 1
audf  55 5 0 1
audf  70 5 0 1
audf  55 5 0 1
audf  70 5 0 1
audf  55 5 0 1
audc  41 40 0 1
amp   0.35
note  The same gesture higher and quicker. In the cabinet this is one bit of difference --
note  SCRSEL D7 -- which is the cheapest characterisation of an enemy in the whole set.

category Impacts

sound Explosion, large rock
audf  180 14 +5 16
audc  143 14 -1 16
amp   0.60
note  The explosion latch is loaded once and then walked down by the frame loop: 50$ reads
note  LEXPSND, DEX, and stores it back until the low six bits are zero. Loaded with $3F
note  that is 63 frames -- a full second of decay -- and because the byte is decremented
note  whole, the volume field D5-D2 steps down once every four frames. Sixteen levels over
note  a second, which is the staircase this fade reproduces.
note  A large rock's pitch bits are 00, the lowest the circuit offers. The sweep finishes
note  on divider 255 exactly -- the last value a byte holds, and one more step would wrap.

sound Explosion, medium rock
audf  60 14 +12 16
audc  143 14 -1 16
amp   0.55
note  Pitch bits 11, the highest of the four. The size byte is EOR'd with 2 and rotated
note  twice into D7-D6 at the 70$ label, which maps large to 00, small to 10 and medium to
note  11 -- so medium, not small, is the highest-pitched explosion in the game.

sound Explosion, small rock
audf  100 14 +9 16
audc  143 14 -1 16
amp   0.50
note  Pitch bits 10. Same volume byte and therefore the same one-second decay as the other
note  two: the game varies the pitch of an explosion by what was destroyed and never its
note  length or its loudness.

sound Ship destroyed
audf  180 14 +5 16
audc  143 14 -1 16
amp   0.70
note  LDX I,3E / STX LEXPSND at the 93$ label. That is the large rock's value less two, so
note  the player's own death is the same pitch, the same volume and two frames shorter than
note  a big rock going up -- a genuinely surprising thing to find in the source. Only the
note  amplitude here separates them, which is this bank editorialising and saying so.

category States

sound Thump, low
audf  220 1 +2 14
audc  170 14 0 1
amp   0.50
note  THUMP1 is loaded with 4 -- four frames on, 64 ms, these 14 ticks -- and then the
note  latch is cleared. The beat itself is the gap, not the sound.

sound Thump, high
audf  170 1 +3 14
audc  170 14 0 1
amp   0.50
note  The pair alternates because the game writes EOR I,14 to LTHUMP: $10 toggles the
note  sound on and $04 flips one frequency bit at the same time, so every other beat is
note  the other tone. Two notes out of one exclusive-or.

sound Extra life
audf  25 308 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
audc  160 28 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
audc  160 28 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
audc  160 28 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
audc  160 28 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
audc  160 28 0 1
audc  170 28 0 1
amp   0.40
note  POINTS loads SND3 with $B0 on every 1000 points, and this is the one sound driven
note  from the NMI rather than the frame loop -- so it is worth following carefully. The
note  handler decrements SND3 whenever FRAME is odd, and it runs four times a frame, so
note  176 counts are spent over 88 frames: 1.4 seconds, which is what these 308 ticks are.
note  The gate bit is FRAME bit 3 (the three RORs after the DEC put it in D7), so the tone
note  is on eight frames and off eight -- 128 ms each way, the 28 ticks per step here, and
note  about five and a half pulses before it stops.
note  The pitch chain never moves. The rhythm is entirely in AUDC, exactly as the
note  original's is entirely in one gate bit.
